'' EXPORT REACHES THE LINKAGE, and until 2026-09-07 it reached nothing. The keyword
'' was consumed by the trailing-modifier loop in decl.bas -- whose own comment listed
'' it among the modifiers it handled -- and recorded nowhere. Measured against fbc on
'' the same source:
''
''     fbc    define dllexport i32 @"AddUp"( ... )
''     tiko   define i32 @"AddUp"( ... )
''
'' A DLL built from that exports nothing, which is why this is the first thing the
'' '-dll' work needs.
''
'' THE BIT MATTERS. PF_EXPORT was first given &h1000, which is PF_GENERIC_PROC. That
'' did not error: the function was read as a generic, generics are not emitted until
'' instantiated, and the module came out with NO FUNCTIONS AT ALL. Only comparing the
'' emitted IR caught it, which is what this test does.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/export.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' The exported one carries dllexport.
'' EXPECT-OUT: define dllexport i32 @"AddUp"(i32 %p0, i32 %p1) nounwind {

'' A PRIVATE procedure stays internal. 'internal dllexport' is not a combination LLVM
'' accepts, and a module-static procedure has no external name to export.
'' EXPECT-OUT: define internal i32 @"HIDDEN"

'' The names are UPPERCASE because FreeBASIC identifiers are case-insensitive and
'' the canonical form is what gets emitted; AddUp keeps its case only because it
'' carries an explicit ALIAS.
''
'' AND AN ORDINARY ONE GAINS NOTHING. Without this the test would pass just as well
'' if dllexport were emitted unconditionally.
'' EXPECT-OUT: define i32 @"PLAIN"
'' EXPECT-NOT: dllexport i32 @"PLAIN"
'' EXPECT-NOT: dllexport i32 @"HIDDEN"
